    "items": [
      {
        "topic": "gate resistors",
        "value": { "rg_on": 3.8, "rg_off": 8.0 },
        "lines": [
          "gate drive taken as 10 V, usual for a Si MOSFET",
          "loop inductance taken as 20 nH for the overshoot limit: give your board's to tighten this",
          "the gate driver's peak current is not considered: give it to include it",
          "turn-on: at least 3.8 ohm in total, set by voltage slew rate under 5 V/ns",
          "turn-on: the transistor's own gate resistance is 1.2 ohm of that, leaving 2.6 ohm for the driver and the external resistor",
          "turn-off: at least 8 ohm in total, set by overshoot within 80% of the 100 V rating",
          "turn-off: the transistor's own gate resistance is 1.2 ohm of that, leaving 6.8 ohm for the driver and the external resistor",
          "at these values a switching event at 19.3 A costs about 16.1 \u00b5J turning on and 16.9 \u00b5J turning off; both grow with the resistor",
          "the results use these values, since you gave none"
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "dead time",
        "value": { "t_dead": 1.474294312574579e-7, "loss_at_common": 4.944546735743722 },
        "lines": [
          "the delay is a low estimate: the gate holds more charge above the plateau than this model knows of, which the margin is there to cover",
          "turning off through 8 ohm takes about 66 ns: 27 ns before anything happens, 24 ns for the voltage to rise, 15 ns for the current to fall (from the gate charges)",
          "a tenth longer when hot, plus 50 ns for the two gate-driver channels not answering together (usual for a half-bridge driver; give yours to tighten this), with a margin of 1.2 times: 147 ns",
          "at least 147 ns",
          "open motor-control firmware often runs 300 to 800 ns by default: at 500 ns the stage would lose 4.94 W, 0.43 W more than at the 150 ns used here"
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "modulation",
        "value": { "saving": 0.7086027045682348 },
        "lines": [
          "space-vector PWM, the usual scheme for this motor: 4.52 W",
          "discontinuous PWM loses 3.81 W, 0.709 W less (16%): it stops switching each leg for part of the cycle",
          "the price is more current ripple and motor noise, most at low speed; drives commonly change to it only above about half speed"
        ]
      },
